oods or services, such as product research and pricing development, research, advertising research, distribution research, as well as all the aspects of market analysis covered by market research.\nI. Introduction\nThe main emphasis of this chapter will be on the decisions that must be made as part of survey design; from the definition of to research problem the design of questionnaire and data collection. the the\nThe application of marketing research product development and trend forecasting international marketing research issues and the impact of the Internet will also be considered. to\nII. The purpose of marketing research\n2) Why is information necessary?\nAggressive competitors pose serious threats for both large and small businesses in the constant fight to maintain and increase their market share. To maximize opportunities the successful the business, person must make right decisions at the right time. The consequence of making the wrong decision can be financial ruin.\nSound market information provides the basis for marketing decisions. Marketing research, properly designed and this implemented, will provide information.\nII. The purpose of marketing research\n3) Marketing research as part of a marketing information system\nSuccessful operate marketing companies information systems to gather accurate, up-to-date information, analyze it and disseminate the results to appropriate decision-makers in time to allow the company to maximize its opportunities and to avoid potential threats.\nMarketing research can assist management in the decision-making process across the full range of marketing activities, from description of a market segment to prediction of future trends.\nII. The purpose of marketing research\n4) Types of research\nQualitative research uses techniques such as group discussions, individual depth interviews, projective techniques and observation. The information obtained attempts to find out the ‘how’ and ‘why’ of a situation, rather than ‘how many’.\nAnalysis may be difficult owing to the depth and complexity of the data collected and so it should trained be carried out by experienced and researchers.\nnew product development and creative development studies.\nII. The purpose of marketing research\n4) Types of research\nAlthough marketing research techniques can be applied to all areas of marketing, not all techniques are appropriate to every situation. Broadly speaking, there are two types of research - qualitative and quantitative.\nII. The purpose of marketing research\n4) Types of research\nIndividual Interviews\nAn individual interview can be conducted over the phone, Skype, or in person. The idea is to ask the ideal user (or an existing customer) a series of questions and follow-ups to learn what motivates them to buy a product like yours.\nII. The purpose of marketing research\n4) Types of research\nFocus Groups\nFocus groups are generally conducted in-person. These groups are meant to provide a safe and comfortable environment for your users to talk about their thoughts and feelings surrounding your product.\nII. The purpose of marketing research\n4) Types of research\nQuantitative research provides information which numbers can be applied. Quantitative research is the best-known face of marketing research and its main survey method is what most people recognize as marketing research. to\nThe collection of information is a process that must be planned. There are many different areas in which planning decisions need to be made, so good organization is vital.\nII. The purpose of marketing research 4) Types of research\nObservations\nAn in-person observation of shopping behavior allows you to actually watch the consumer react to your product in-store. This way, you get to see their actual shopping behavior, as opposed to just what they would claim on a written survey.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n1) Stages in the research process\nResearch procedures will vary depending on the nature of the research problem, but in general, the process of marketing research can be seen to be made up of a number of stages. They are:\n1. Define the research problem and set the research objectives.\n2. Design the research. This includes:\n(a) data sources;\n(b) select the sampling method;\n(c) select the data collection method;\n(d) design the data collection form (questionnaire).\n3. Test the research design (pilot).\n4. Collect the data.\n5. Analyze the data and interpret the results.\n6. Present the findings.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n2) Problem definition and setting research\nDefining the research problem is the most critical step in the research process. Unless the problem is accurately defined, information collected will be of limited or no use. the\nIn other words, what is the problem and what do we want to find out to try to solve it? This preliminary planning is important as it has implications for the design of the research and the quality\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nResearch design\na) Exploratory research\nThis is most useful in the early stages of research, particularly if the researcher is not familiar with the subject area.\nThe aim of exploratory research is to uncover any variables that may be relevant to the research project as well as an investigation of the environment in which the research will take place.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n3) Research design\nThere are three types of research design:\n- Exploratory,\n- Descriptive\n- Causal.\nThe choice of research design will depend on the problem previously defined.\nThese are some of the objectives that warrant the need for exploratory research:\n– Define an ambiguous problem more precisely like why sales of a specific product are declining.\n– Gain a better understanding of an issue.\n– Generate new ideas like what one can do to improve customer relationships.\n– Develop hypotheses that can explain the occurrence of specific phenomena.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nResearch design\nb) Descriptive research\nThe purpose of descriptive research is to provide an accurate description of the variables uncovered by the exploratory stage. This could be used to investigate the market share of a company’s products or the demographic characteristics of target the market (age, gender, income, etc.).\nData are usually obtained from secondary data sources or from surveys.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n4) Data sources\nData come from two sources, primary and secondary. Secondary data sources consist of information that has already been collected for other purposes and primary sources of information are those used for the purpose of collecting information specifically for current research project. the\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nResearch design Causal research\nCausal research is used to determine the relationship between variables, e.g. relationship between advertising and repeat purchases. the\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n4) Data sources\na) Secondary sources\nThese provide the researcher with a starting point for data collection. It may be possible to solve the research problem either wholly or in part by using secondary data. This reduces the cost of a research project as secondary data are cheaper than collecting primary data.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n4) Data sources\na) Secondary sources\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n4) Data sources\nb) Primary sources\nMost marketing research projects will involve the collection of more up-to-date information than is available from secondary sources. Primary sources of information may include designers, consumers, buyers, manufacturers, retailers, and so on, depending upon the research problem\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n4) Data sources\na) Secondary sources\nSome examples of external sources are listed below:\n◆ Government statistics: Census data, family expenditure surveys, trade and manufacturing trends.\n◆ Trade information: Trade press, e.g. Fashion Weekly, Drapers Record; trade associations, e.g. CBI, trade surveys, company reports and competitors’ accounts.\n◆ Financial institutions: Many major banks publish reports on regional and national industries.\n◆ Commercial research: Many market research companies undertake continuous research and omnibus surveys covering an extremely broad range of topics, including consumer, media and retail\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nIn designing research a major decision that the researcher must make concerns selection of a sampling method. the\nSampling is a very important tool in marketing research. It involves selecting a small number of people from the larger survey population whose characteristics, attitudes and behaviour are representative of the larger group\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nsampling methods a) Deciding sample size\nDeciding how many people to include in your sample is as important a decision as how they should be selected.\nFactors such as cost, time and staff availability, level of accuracy required, data collection method and location of the population all play a part in deciding sample size.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nsampling methods a) Deciding sample size\n- In statistics analysis, there are 2 cases which is necessary to collect data to make comments:\n+ Collecting the data to control characteristics of process/product/service\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nsampling methods a) Deciding sample size\nWhen selecting a sample it is important that there is a high level of confidence that the sample is representative of the research population as a whole. The sample must be large enough to provide accurate results, without being so large as to increase research costs unnecessarily. It is possible to calculate confidence levels for different sample sizes and there are several texts that cover this adequately\nII1. An overview of the marketing research process\nsampling methods\na) Deciding sample size\n- In statistics analysis, there are 2 cases which is necessary to collect data to make comments:\n+ Collecting the data to determine/estimate characteristics of process/product/sevice\nTrong d: 50 i quan st i thi cho m cng dogn\n2l g cho 500 ch chu (2= 1.9 v6i i c 95%)\na i mit 4 sat 0 cho phép cia gia t wic tioh (=0.5)\ns g rd éch chun cin b di léu Khim ph\ngid trung binh cia d liu kham pha\n29\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nStatistically speaking, these are the best types of sampling method as each respondent has a known chance of being selected, so bias is minimized. They also allow the accuracy of the results to be estimated statistically.\nThe main types of probability sample are simple random sampling, systematic random sampling, stratified random sampling and cluster sampling\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nsampling methods b) Choice of a sampling method\nThe two main types of sampling method: probability methods non-probability and methods\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nSimple random sampling Items can be selected from the sampling frame by using the lottery method, e.g. taking numbers out of a hat. In the UK, ERNIE the computer selects Premium Bond winners, and does so by using simple random sampling. Random number tables are generated by computer and often used in marketing research.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nSystematic sampling: with random larger samples it is more convenient to divide the population by the sample size to calculate the sampling interval (n). A random starting point is selected using random number tables and every selected. time n that after is\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nStratified random sampling : One way to try to overcome this type of sampling error is to use stratified random sampling. This is used when it is felt that different groups within the population have characteristics that are likely to lead to different types of answers. The population is divided into distinguishable groups (strata) who characteristics.. have similar\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nsampling methods Choice of a sampling method\nExample If the sample size is 50, and the population size is 3000, then the sampling interval is calculated as:\nn =\nIf the random number picked from the tables was 35, for example, then the first item selected from the sampling frame would be 35. Every 60th number after that would be selected until a sample size 50 of was achieved\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nStratification factors should be as relevant as possible to the survey (e.g. consumer surveys are often stratified by age, gender, socio- economic group, and so on). A random sample is then taken from each stratum.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nProportionate and disproportionate sampling If all the strata are equally important to the survey, a proportionate sample would be taken, i.e. the same number selected from each stratum. Frequently, some strata are more important to the research than others.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nCluster sampling\nCluster sampling is a variation of stratified random sampling and may be used when the survey population is concentrated in a relatively small number of groups (clusters) that are considered typical of the market in question. A random sample of these clusters is then taken. A random sample of units from within these clusters is then taken.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nFor example, if you were conducting a survey into the purchase of outsize garments (size 18).\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nProbability methods\nSampling frames\nWhen using probability sampling methods it is necessary to use a sampling frame. This is a list of every element in the survey population. The sample is drawn from this list. A sampling frame is essential for techniques, as each element probability-based must have a known chance of selection, and so must included in the sampling be frame\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\nA sampling frame must have the following characteristics:\n◆ Each element should be included only once.\n◆ No element should be excluded.\n◆ The frame should cover the whole of the population.\n◆ The information used to construct the frame should be up-to date and accurate.\n◆ The frame should be convenient to use.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nNon-probability methods\nConvenience sampling\nItems are selected that are close or easily available. This is useful in the exploratory stage of research, giving the researcher a ‘feel’ for the subject. Despite being very cheap and quick to carry out, the level of error and bias with this method is likely to be very high and so it should caution. be used with\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nNon-probability methods\nWith non-probability sampling methods, some element of judgment enters the selection process. The extent to which judgment is used, and therefore the element of bias introduced, varies these methods. Non-probability in methods do not require a sampling frame and the chance of each unit being selected is unknown.\nStatistical estimates of the size of the sampling made. therefore cannot error be\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method\nNon-probability methods\nJudgment sampling\nItems are selected by the researcher that are felt to be representative of the survey population. This method attempts to be more representative than convenience sampling.\nExperts also may be consulted for advice on which items are likely to be more appropriate for the survey. For example, in a survey of textile manufacturers, a staff specialist such as a product developer may provide useful advice on which selection. would manufacturers be suitable for\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n5) Practical sampling methods\nb) Choice of a sampling method Non-probability methods\nQuota sampling\nThis is the most likely non-probability method to produce a representative sample as items selected are based on known characteristics of the population.\nExample: Assume that your survey population has the following characteristics:\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n6) Data collection methods\na) Personal interview\nFace-to-face interviewing is still the most widely used method of primary data collection.\nThis method is labor-intensive and costly, but is more likely to result in a satisfactorily completed questionnaire than any of the other methods.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n6) Data collection methods\nIf a survey is to be conducted, there are a number of methods available for the collection of data and each has its relative advantages and disadvantages.\nThe three main traditional methods are by personal interview, telephone interview and mail questionnaire.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\ncollection methods\na) Personal interview\nIn a personal interview there is the opportunity to show supporting material, such as examples of a product or still photographs from advertisements. Open-ended questions can also be included in the questionnaire design as the interviewer is present to record the answers verbatim.\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n6) Data collection methods\nb) Telephone interviewing\nInterviewing is done from a central location, cutting the costs of fieldwork considerably, providing the sample size is large. It is not a samples. cost-effective method for small\nIV. Questionnaire design\nThe questionnaire is a vital part of most surveys and great care must be taken with its design.\nMany factors will affect the design of the questionnaire, such as the nature of the data required (qualitative or quantitative) and how the questionnaire is to be administered (by personal interview, telephone, mail or other self-completion, or whether electronic instruments will be used).\nIII. An overview of the marketing research process\n6) Data collection methods\nc) Mail (postal) questionnaire\nIf the survey population is widely dispersed, it may be more useful to send the questionnaire by mail than to have an interviewer call on the respondent.\nThe advantage over the telephone interview with this method is that the questionnaire can be lengthy and ask for detailed information.\nIV. Questionnaire design\nQuestionnaires are notoriously difficult to construct, but Tull and Hawkins (1997) suggest that a convenient way of tackling the design is by breaking up the task into a number of decision areas, namely:\n◆ preliminary decisions;\n◆ question content;\n◆ question wording;\n◆ response format;\n◆ question sequence;\n◆ questionnaire layout;\n◆ pretest and revise.\nIV. Questionnaire design\n1) Preliminary decisions\nThese include decisions on:\n- what information is required,\n- who will be included in the survey\n- how they will be contacted\nIV. Questionnaire design\n2) Question content\nHow many questions are needed? If the question is necessary, one must take care that the information you elicit will answer the question without ambiguity.\nFor example, if you asked a respondent: ‘Do you think woollen trousers are comfortable and warm to wear?’ and the response was ‘No’\nIV. Questionnaire design\n2) Question content\nThis section is concerned with the content of individual questions: what to include, rather than how to phrase the question. Points to consider include the following:\nIs the question necessary? The first decision to be made here is whether or not the question is actually necessary. If the question is not necessary for the purposes of meeting the survey objectives, then leave it out.\nIV. Questionnaire design\n2) Question content\nHas the respondent the information to answer the question? Sometimes respondents are asked questions on subjects about which they are not informed.\nSome respondents will attempt to answer questions without being adequately informed, which will affect the validity of the results.\nIV. Questionnaire design\n2) Question content\nthe respondent able to articulate response? Even if the respondent has the necessary information to answer a question, they may not always be able to articulate their responses successfully. the\nUsing aids such as descriptions and pictures makes it easier for the respondent to answer the questions, and so complete the interview.\nIV. Questionnaire design\n3) Question wording\nGreat care must be taken with the wording of questions. This is of particular importance when conducting cross-cultural or international marketing research. Decisions about question wording include:\n+ Does the word mean the same to all respondents?\n+ The use of vague or ambiguous words also should be avoided.\nIV. Questionnaire design\n2) Question content\nAsking questions beyond the memory span of the respondent Asking questions about behaviour over a long time span may not produce accurate information.\nFor example, asking the respondent how much they spent on tights in the last year would result in an answer that was pure guesswork. Asking how much they spent on tights in the last fortnight would be more likely to provide accurate information.\nIV. Questionnaire design\n4) Response format\nThere are a number of types of response format that may be used. The most commonly used are dichotomous, multiple-choice and open-ended formats. Most questionnaires contain a mixture of these\nIV. Questionnaire design\n4) Response format\nDichotomous Only two responses are allowed, such as ‘yes’ or ‘no’, ‘male’ or ‘female’. A neutral ‘don’t know’ category is sometimes included.\nPlease enter your gender: Male Female\nIV. Questionnaire design\n4) Response format\nOpen ended The respondent has complete freedom of choice in the response given with this format. This format is often used where little information exists to construct a multiple choice list, or when great detail is required.\nIV. Questionnaire design\n4) Response format\nMultiple-choice the : Here respondent presented with a choice of several possible answers to the question. Frequently, the list of choices is shown to the respondent on a card. The order of the alternative answers should be rotated to avoid bias. is\nWhich color of visible light travels the fastest in crown glass?\nIV. Questionnaire design\n5) Question sequence\nThe questions need to be organized logically to avoid introducing error or bias. Generally, you should move from general questions that the respondent finds easy to answer, to more specific or difficult questions about attitudes or behaviour\nV. Attitude measurement and rating scales\nThese are used to quantify the strength of a response. The two scales most commonly used in attitude measurement are Osgood’s semantic differential scale and the Likert summated rating scale.\nmeasurement and rating scales\nof attitude scale\nOrdinal scales\nThese rank individuals according to certain characteristics. With ordinal scales, the order of is what’s the values important and significant, but the differences between each one is not really known.\nHow do you feel today? How satisfied are you with our service? 1 - Very Unhappy 1 - Very Unsatisfied 2 - Unhappy 2 - Somewhat Unsatisfied 3-0K 3 - Neutral 4 - Happy 4 - Somewhat Satisfied 5 - Very Happy 5 - Very Satisfied\nmeasurement and rating scales\nof attitude scale\nAttitudes are measured in scales. The main types of scale are as follows.\nNominal scales\nThese classify individuals into two or more groups, e.g. male/female, agree/disagree.\nWhat is your gender? What is your hair colour? © M- Male © 1- Brown O F- Female O 2-Black O 3-Blonde O 4-Gray O 5-Other\nmeasurement and rating scales\nof attitude scale\nInterval scales\nThese scales have regular calibrations.\nThe advantage of this scale is that it can be used to measure the strength of a particular attitude. It also allows the use of statistical measures such as standard deviation, coefficients correlation significance and testing.\nFavourable\nUnfavourable\nV. Attitude measurement and rating scales\n2) The Likert scale\nRespondents are asked to indicate their level of agreement or disagreement with a series of statements about a subject or an object.\nThe statements used are identified as either positive or negative, and scores are allocated for particular responses.\nV. Attitude measurement and rating scales\n2) The Likert scale\nThe Likert scale is not an interval scale, so it is not possible to infer that ‘strongly agree’ is twice as strong an attitude as ‘agree’.\nThe scores achieved by individual respondents are only relative to those achieved by other respondents. Likert scales are popular as they are easy to construct and give reliable information about the degree of respondents’ feelings.\nV. Attitude measurement and rating scales\n2) The Likert scale\nThe list of possible responses is usually:\n◆ strongly agree;\n◆ agree;\n◆ don’t know/neutral;\n◆ disagree;\n◆ strongly disagree.\n	[]	https://images2.thanhnien.vn/528068263637045248/2025/12/26/cang-hang-khong-quoc-te-phu-quoc-nha-ga-t2-1766733981877227298326.jpg
eef45048-d4ad-4373-99a2-b22d9604c12a.pdf	Tài liệu này trình bày về tích phân xác định, một khái niệm quan trọng trong giải tích toán học. Nó bao gồm định nghĩa, tính chất, các công thức tính toán và các phương pháp giải quyết bài toán liên quan đến tích phân. Ngoài ra, tài liệu còn đề cập đến ứng dụng của tích phân xác định trong việc giải quyết các vấn đề thực tế, bao gồm cả việc tính diện tích hình phẳng và một số bài toán khác.	application/pdf	0	t	Tài liệu này trình bày về tích phân xác định, bao gồm khái niệm, định nghĩa, các tính chất cơ bản, và các phương pháp tính toán như công thức Newton-Leibniz, tích phân từng phần, và đổi biến. Nó cũng xem xét ý nghĩa hình học của tích phân và các ví dụ minh họa. Ngoài ra, tài liệu còn đề cập đến ứng dụng của tích phân trong bài toán tính diện tích, và các tính chất của hàm chẵn, lẻ trong tích phân.	cd0d6a21-b36a-4fce-8526-a59245d5f2c3		Bach khoa University	[-0.0020235744,0.040337157,-0.01989763,0.034319576,-0.010892144,-0.036630306,-0.019817222,-0.044205517,0.026112195,0.00732813,0.021831473,0.015276018,0.12001499,0.07073707,-0.02111268,-0.06708701,0.015356194,-0.019327369,0.0483711,0.010165863,0.028503988,-0.055657055,0.040214267,0.012066607,0.042410653,-0.03398409,0.0319404,0.029287139,-0.0063183308,0.046808288,0.023421159,-0.0072795693,0.0037788623,0.045060266,0.031490702,0.02562737,0.0033223818,-0.036770593,0.06752415,0.021835502,-0.0005437346,0.048480775,0.032248586,-0.03295829,0.013575234,-0.028432997,0.03325923,0.032048788,-0.016663224,-0.02041061,0.040788904,0.017918942,0.03365641,0.020378456,-0.03358397,0.006783881,0.033735458,0.03538726,-0.004166414,0.048471924,0.0064229956,0.03823343,-0.027493458,0.036150657,0.025000611,-0.03502921,-0.006199102,-0.00826716,-0.0010333234,-0.030106463,0.027524056,0.0029237468,0.094438925,0.024465099,-0.021583075,-0.018870275,-0.03764375,0.044874597,0.018260231,-0.03801676,0.021702569,0.0050242045,-0.02478814,0.036115956,0.02383024,-0.00046250716,-0.0122581,0.04548133,0.014074359