3505,-0.10032773,-0.021001952,-0.01060443,-0.023148498,0.039186854,0.02173181,0.018766217,-0.03452294,-0.005183164,-0.031375032,-0.04895155,0.0014698836,0.038390037,-0.006982409,-0.017185548,-0.061302703,-0.013038789,0.056291528,0.03130543,-0.04959249,0.018610124,0.016798155,0.035407312,0.030982787,-0.008487498,0.043186847,-0.016775707,-0.047568265,0.049855165]	Chapter 7 Cookies and Sessions\nLectured by: Nguyễn Hữu Hiếu\nThe need for persistence\nl Consider these examples\n− Counting the number of “hits” on a website\n− i.e. how many times does a client load your web page source\n− The questionnaire on computing experience\nl Somehow your .php needs to remember previous instances of it being requested by a client\nPersistence\nl Persistence is the ability of data to outlive the execution of the program that created them.\nl An obvious way of achieving persistence is to simply save the data in a file\nPersistence and HTTP\nRecall http is a stateless protocol. It remembers nothing about previous transfers\nTwo ways to achieve persistence:\nl PHP cookies\nl PHP sessions\nWl\nHTTP Cookies\nIn internet programming, a cookie is a packet of information sent from the server to client, and then sent back to the server each time it is accessed by the client.\nIntroduces state into HTTP (remember: HTTP is stateless)\nCookies are transferred between server and client according to http.\nPHP supports http cookies\nCookies can also be thought of as tickets used to identify clients and their orders\nWe\nHow Cookies are implemented\nl Cookies are sent from the server to the client via “Set- Cookie” headers\nSet-Cookie:NAME=VALUE; expires=DATE; path=PATH; domain=DOMAIN_NAME; secure\nl The NAME value is a URL-encoded name that identifies the cookie.\nl The PATH and DOMAIN specify where the cookie applies\nsetcookie(name,value,expire,path,domain,secure)\n\n\n=== [BẢNG SỐ LIỆU] ===\nParameter Description (Required). Specifies the name of the cookie name value (Required). Specifies the value of the cookie expire (Optional). Specifies when the cookie expires. e.g. time()+3600*24*30 will set the cookie to expire in 30 days. If this parameter is not set, the cookie will expire at the end of the session (when the browser closes). path (Optional). Specifies the server path of the cookie. If set to "/", the cookie will be available within the entire domain. If set to "/phptest/", the cookie will only be available within the test directory and all sub- directories of phptest. The default value is the current directory that the cookie is being set in. domain (Optional). Specifies the domain name of the cookie. To make the cookie available on all subdomains of example.com then you'd set it to ".example.com". Setting it to www.example.com will make the cookie only available in the www subdomain secure (Optional). Specifies whether or not the cookie should only be transmitted over a secure HTTPS connection. TRUE indicates that the cookie will only be set if a secure connection exists. Default is FALSE.\n======================\nCookies from HTTP\nClient (e.g. Firefox)\nit026945\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK Content-type: text/html Set-Cookie: name=value\n(content of page)\nCreating PHP cookies\nCookies can be set by directly manipulating the HTTP header using the PHP header() function\nCreating cookies with setcookie()\nUse the PHP setcookie() function:\nSetcookie (name,value,expire, path, domain, secure) e.g.\nl Name: name of the file\nl Value: data stored in the file\nl Expire: data string defining the life time\nl Path: subset of URLs in a domain where it is valid\nl Domain: domain for which the cookie is valid\nl Secure: set to '1' to transmit in HTTPS\nReading cookies\nTo access a cookie received from a client, use the PHP $_COOKIE superglobal array\nEach key in the array represents a cookie - the key name is the cookie name.\nCreating and using cookies example\nl Cookies only become visible on the next page load\nUsing headers (wrong approach!)\nUsing headers\nn setcookie() did not run before information was sent to the browser...\nn Cookies have to be sent beforethe heading elements\nW\nUsing headers (correct approach)\nDeleting a cookie\nl Set the cookie with its name only:\nsetcookie(“mycookie”);\nMultiple data items\n© 2017\nWhere is the cookie stored\nl Depends on the browser...\nl e.g., firefox/mozilla under /home/a________\n− Look for cookies.txt in .mozilla directory\n− Usually under:\nl /home/a______/.mozilla/firefox/asdkfljy.default\n− Cookie is stored only if there is an expiry date\n− Otherwise it is deleted when leaving browser\n− Persistentonly if an expiry date is set\nPHP Sessions\nPHP Sessions\nYou can store user information (e.g. username, items selected, etc.) in the server side for later use using PHP session.\nSessions work by creating a unique id (UID) for each visitor and storing variables based on this UID.\nThe UID is either stored in a cookie or is propagated in the URL.\nWhen should you use sessions?\nl Need for data to stored on the server\nl Unique session information for each user\nl Transient data, only relevant for short time\nl Data does not contain secret information\nl Similar to Cookies, but it is stored on the server\nl More secure, once established, no data is sent back and forth between the machines\nl Works even if cookies are disabled\nl Example: we want to count the number of “hits” on our web page.\nBefore you can store user information in your PHP session, you must first start up the session.\nsession_start() function must appear BEFORE the <html> tag.\nPHP Sessions\nStarting a PHP session:\n• This tells PHP that a session is requested.\n• A session ID is then allocated at the server end.\n• session ID looks like:\nsess_f1234781237468123768asjkhfa7891234g\nl $_SESSION\nl e.g., $_SESSION[“intVar”] = 10;\nSession variables l Testing if a session variable has been set: session_start();\nif(!$_SESSION['intVar']) {...} //intVar is set or not\nRegistering session variables\nl Instead of setting superglobals, one can register one’s own session variables\n• $barney can now be accessed “globally” from session to session\nl This only works if the register_globalsdirective is enabled in php.ini - nowadays this is turned off by default\nMake your own session variables\nl With session_start() a default session variable is created - the name extracted from the page name\nl To create your own session variable just add a new key to the $_SESSION superglobal\n$_SESSION[‘dug’] = “a talking dog.”;\nLập Trình Web\n27\nSession Example 1\nSession Example 2\nEnding sessions\nunset($_SESSION[‘name’])\n–Remove a session variable\nsession_destroy()\n– Destroys all data registered to a session\n– does not unset session global variables and cookies associated with the session\n–Not normally done - leave to timeout\nDestroying a session completely\n31\nSession Example 3\nTrường Đại Học BáchKhoa TP.HCM\nKhoa Khoa Học và Kỹ Thuật Máy Tính\n© 2017\nSession Example 3 (cont.)\nExamples\nphp_imagefields.php n\nphp_retention.php n\nupload.html n\nupload.php n\nphp_imagefields.php n\nphp_imagecreation.php n\nphp_truetypefonts.php n\nphp_file_get_contents.ph n p\nphp_cookie_multipledata. n php\ncookie1.php n\ncookie_with_headers.php n\nsession1.php\nsession2.php\nphp_session_colours2.php\nphp_session_destroy.php\nSummary\nPHP sessions and cookies are mechanisms for introducing state into HTTP transactions.\nTài Liệu Tham Khảo\nn [1] Stepp,Miller,Kirst. Web Programming Step by Step.( 1st Edition, 2009) Companion Website: http://www.webstepbook.com/\nn [2] W3Schools, http://www.w3schools.com/html/default.asp\n	[]	https://images2.thanhnien.vn/528068263637045248/2025/12/26/cang-hang-khong-quoc-te-phu-quoc-nha-ga-t2-1766733981877227298326.jpg
d9721092-8a7e-4b3e-8143-f7133b088275.pdf	Chương 9 Lập trình web	application/pdf	0	f	This document provides an introduction to Search Engine Optimization (SEO), covering key aspects such as keywords, on-page optimization (meta tags, header tags, image optimization, SEO-friendly URLs), off-page SEO (backlinks, content marketing, social media), and technical SEO elements like sitemaps and robots.txt.  It explains how search engines work, including PageRank and SERP, and emphasizes the importance of quality content and link building for improving search rankings and website visibility. It also touches on related topics like Smart Card Reader.	09cde5ba-635c-43b6-b2b2-0c73da21f14a	LTW	Bách Khoa	[0.019616717,0.047296256,-0.013022911,0.02355682,0.048626114,-0.07083419,-0.035443023,-0.023270082,0.032484945,0.008252816,-0.038805746,0.030335894,0.12909932,0.036748253,-0.014978847,-0.052675717,0.033247095,-0.010428377,0.032288197,-0.02300818,0.04668593,-0.019812338,0.05121086,-0.014917803,0.011493929,-0.031422466,0.03027341,0.008473422,-0.033665,0.048755687,0.039097585,-0.05015702,0.005873492,0.014456701,0.034653053,0.014843594,-0.018148176,-0.026664814,0.013592272,0.05527768,0.022050811,0.03134133,-0.0065837204,-0.06840211,0.019300131,-0.036911838,0.033732664,0.026793225,-0.019435823,-0.015101594,0.021400763,0.0008212689,0.028106056,0.033507515,-0.016541226,-0.040761866,0.02541507,0.03984437,0.0017101314,0.04305199,-0.028843941,0.016809795,0.0021731209,0.045810387,0.07232418,-0.042042144,0.011924292,-0.024829093,-0.03143353,-0.01931797,0.039990053,-0.027193097,0.06028514,-0.01603094,-0.04427634,-0.010911824,-0.024271218,0.03553415,0.011415653,-0.0001751194,0.035047755,0.021301705,-0.0035899014,0.04318668,0.005193695,-0.0020452691,-0.027239129,0.016044032,0.044896644,0.0051833685,0.012890366,-0.03524377,-0.035454415,0.009572261,0.040370826,0.0327429,0.011147253,0.0005241441,0.028018875,-0.029820843,-0.020955903,-0.028047414,-0.037203625,-0.06386807,-0.025566855,0.012448497,-0.0