ation time proportional to n2k.\n• A mature technology.\nContents\neast Sauares Model\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nUsing least-squares\n• Basis for regression analysis, optimal control, and many parameter estimation and data fitting methods.\n• Easy to recognize.\n• A few standard techniques increase flexibility\n• Including weights\n• Adding regularization terms\nContents\neast Sauares Model\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nData fitting\nGeneral statement\nIn a fitting problem, we are given data\nwith ui ∈ D and yi ∈ R, and seek a function f ∈ F that matches this data as closely as possible. For example in least-squares fitting we consider the problem\nwhich is a simple least-squares problem in the variable x.\nWe can add a variety of constraints\n• inequalities that must be satisfied by f at various points,\n• constraints on the derivatives of f,\n• monotonicity constraints,\n• or moment constraints.\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nmm\nRegression analysis and data fitting\nTrend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nData fitting\nPolynomial fitting\nGiven data u1,u1,...,um ∈ R and v1,v2,...,vm ∈ R, we need to approximately fit a polynomial of the form\nto the data.\nFor each x we form the vector of errors,\nTo find the polynomial that minimizes the norm of the error, we solve the norm approximation problem\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nmm\nRegression analysis and data fitting\nTrend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nwhere x ∈ Rn, Aij = uj−1 i , for i = 1,2,...,m and j = 1,2,...,n\nPolynomial fitting\nConvex Optimization in Machine Learning\nContents\nRegression analysis and data fitting\nTrend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nTrend filter: time series analysis\n• The problem of estimating underlying trends in time series data arises in a variety of disciplines.\n• The l1 trend filtering method produces trend estimates x that are piecewise linear from the time series y.\nOptimization form\nGiven a time series data y, estimate x by solving\nwhere D is the second difference matrix, with rows [0... − 1 2 − 1...0], λ > 0 is the regularization parameter.\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\ndata ftting. Regression anysis and T\nTrend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nTrend filter: time series analysis\nConvex Optimization in Machine Learning\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting\nT\nTrend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nOutline\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\n@ Support Vector Machines\nHard margin Soft margin Kernels\n3 Image Restoration\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\n4 How about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nClassification problem\nA statement\nA classification problem has two types of variables,\n• xi ∈ Rd: the vector of observations (features) in the world,\n• yi ∈ Rd: state (class) vector of the world.\nThe set of all samples in this world (or dataset) constructs the observation set X ∈ Rn×d and state set Y ∈ Rn×d. The task of machine learning algorithm for classification is to find a mapping X → Y that achieves smallest number of misclassification.\nConvex Optimization i Machine Learning\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\ne\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nLinear discriminant learning\n• Propose a parametric family of decision boundaries, then pick the element in this family that produces the best classifier.\n• Take our decision boundary between two classes as a hyperplane, such that wTx + b = 0,\n• w being the normal to the plane and b being the bias term.\n• The decision function can be expressed as\nThe decision boundary\n® Normal vector: w; distance to origin: d = b/ ||w]|.\ne distance to a data point ;: |w; +b|/ |w].\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\ne\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nLinear discriminant learning\nThe decision function\n• Use labels y ∈ {−1,1} instead of y ∈ {0,1}\nA simple necessary and sufficient condition for a given training set D = (x1,y1),(x2,y2),...,(xn,yn), is linearly separable, i.e.,\nConvex Optimization ir Machine Learning\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\n-\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nKernel-based learning\nQuestion arising when dealing with nonlinear problems\n• Trying to fit a nonlinear model? – not easy!\n• Idea: keep using the linear model\n• Map the problem a new (higher-dimensional) space (called the feature space) by doing a nonlinear transformation using suitably chosen basis functions;\n• Apply a linear model in the feature space.\n• Learning a linear boundary in a higher dimensional space will be equivalent to learning a nonlinear boundary in the current space.\n→ Known as the kernel trick.\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\ne\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nKernel-based learning\n• If we introduce a mapping φ : X → Z such that dim(Z) > dim(X), we can achieve linear separability for some k = dim(Z).\n• Instead of applying this mapping to each data point as φ(x), we can leverage the dot-product form\nThen we apply the kernel trick, K(x,z) = φ(x)Tφ(z).\n• Some formulas for K(x,z)\n1 K(x,z) = xTz, linear kernel.\nI e O K(z,z)=¢e 5 , Gaussian kernel.\n3 K(x,z) = (1 + xTz)k, polynomial kernel.\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nSupport Vector Machines (SVM)\n• SVMs go one step further from the necessary and sufficient condition given by (1) and works on the margin defined by the distance from the boundary to the closest point,\n• Maximizing γ is ill-defined, since γ does not change if both w and b are scaled by a factor λ. We then need some sort of normalization,\n® this can be done by arbitrarily selecting some normalization onw, eg. w||=1\n® The SVM algorithm works with a more convenient normalization and makes b| =1 for the closest point, i.e. min; +b under y = [\nSVM model\nContents\nLeast Squares Model\nRegression anysis and data ftting.\nTrend analysis e\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nImage Restoration\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nSVM – hard margin\nConvex Optimization in Machine Learning\nContents\nRegression analysis and data fitting Trend analysis\nSupport Vector Machines\nHard margin\nSoft margin\nKernels\nHow about Deep Networks? – Nonlinear Optimization!\nSVM – soft margin\nConvex Optimization in Machine Learning\nContents\nSoft Margin