Provide a useful perspective for understanding many learning algorithms that do not explicitly manipulate probabilities.\nFeatures of Bayesian Learning\n• Each training example can incrementally decrease or increase the estimated probability that a hypothesis is correct.\n• Prior knowledge can be combined with observed data to determine the ﬁnal probability of a hypothesis\n• Hypotheses with probabilities can be accommodated\n• New instances can be classiﬁed by combining multiple hypotheses weighted by the probabilities.\nBayes Theorem\nBayes Theorem\n• Maximum A-posteriori hypothesis (MAP):\nP(h) is not a uniform distribution over H.\nBayes Theorem\n• Maximum Likelihood hypothesis (ML):\nIf P(h) is a uniform distribution over H.\nBayes Theorem\n• 0.008 of the population have cancer\nBayes Theorem\n• 0.008 of the population have cancer\n• Only 98% patients are correctly classiﬁed as positive\nBayes Theorem\n• 0.008 of the population have cancer\n• Only 98% patients are correctly classiﬁed as positive\n• Only 97% non-patiants are correctly classiﬁed as negative\nBayes Theorem\n• 0.008 of the population have cancer\n• Only 98% patients are correctly classiﬁed as positive\n• Only 97% non-patiants are correctly classiﬁed as negative\n• Would a person with a positive result have cancer or not?\nBayes Theorem\n• Maximum A-posteriori hypothesis (MAP):\nBayes Theorem\n• P(cancer) = .008 → P(¬cancer) = .992\nBayes Theorem\n• P(cancer) = .008 → P(¬cancer) = .992\n• P(⊕|cancer) = .98\nBayes Theorem\n• P(cancer) = .008 → P(¬cancer) = .992\n• P(⊕|cancer) = .98\no P(|cancer) = .97 — P(®|cancer) = .03\nBayes Theorem\n• P(cancer) = .008 → P(¬cancer) = .992\n• P(⊕|cancer) = .98\n(] P(6|cancer) = .97 — P(®|-cancer) = .03\n• P(cancer|⊕) ≈ P(⊕|cancer)p(cancer) = .0078\nBayes Theorem\n• P(cancer) = .008 → P(¬cancer) = .992\n• P(⊕|cancer) = .98\n(] P(6|cancer) = .97 — P(®|-cancer) = .03\n• P(cancer|⊕) ≈ P(⊕|cancer)p(cancer) = .0078\n• P(¬cancer|⊕) ≈ P(⊕|¬cancer)P(¬cancer) = .0298\nBayes Theorem\n• Maximum A-posteriori hypothesis (MAP):\nBayes Optimal Classiﬁer\n• What is the most probable hypothesis given the training data?\n• What is the most probable classiﬁcation of a new instance given the training data?\nBayes Optimal Classiﬁer\n• Hypothesis space = {h1,h2,h3}\n• Posterior probabilities = {.4,.3,.3} (h1 is hMAP)\n• New instance x is classiﬁed positive by h1 and negative by h2 and h3\nWhat is the most probable classiﬁcation of x?\nBayes Optimal Classiﬁer\n• The most probable classiﬁcation of a new instance is obtained by combining the predictions of all hypotheses weighted by their posterior probabilities:\n\n\n=== [BẢNG SỐ LIỆU] ===\nExample Sky AirTemp Humidity Wind Water Forecast EnjoySport 1 Sunny Warm Normal Strong Warm Same Yes 2 Sunny Warm High Strong Warm Same Yes 3 Rainy Cold High Strong Warm Change No 4 Sunny Warm High Strong Cool Change Yes 5 Cloudy Warm High Weak Cool Same Yes 6 Sunny Cold High Weak Cool Same No 7 Sunny Warm Normal Strong Warm Same ? 8 Sunny Warm Low Strong Cool Same ?\n======================\nNaive Bayes Classiﬁer\n• Each instance x is described by a conjunction of attribute values < a1,a2,...,an >\n• The target function f(x) can take on any value from a ﬁnite set C\n• It is to assign the most probable target value to a new instance\nNaive Bayes Classiﬁer\nNaive Bayes Classiﬁer\nassuming that a1,a2,...,an are independent given c\n\n\n=== [BẢNG SỐ LIỆU] ===\nExample Sky AirTemp Humidity Wind Water Forecast EnjoySport 1 Sunny Warm Normal Strong Warm Same Yes 2 Sunny Warm High Strong Warm Same Yes 3 Rainy Cold High Strong Warm Change No 4 Sunny Warm High Strong Cool Change Yes 5 Cloudy Warm High Weak Cool Same Yes 6 Sunny Cold High Weak Cool Same No 7 Sunny Warm Normal Strong Warm Same ? 8 Sunny Warm Low Strong Cool Same ?\n======================\nNaive Bayes Classiﬁer\nEstimating probabilities:\n• Probability: the fraction of times the event is observed to occur over the total number of opportunities nc/n\n• What if the fraction is too small, or even zero?\nNaive Bayes Classiﬁer\nEstimating probabilities:\n• n: total number of training examples of a particular class.\n• nc: number of training examples having a particular attribute value in that class.\n• m: equivalent sample size\n• p: prior estimate of the probability (equals 1/k where k is the number of possible values of the attribute)\nNaive Bayes Classiﬁer\nLearning to classify text:\nNaive Bayes Classiﬁer\nLearning to classify text:\nassuming that all words have equal chance occurring in every position\n	machine-learning bayesian-learning linear-prediction bayes-theorem naive-bayes	http://localhost:8888/api/v1/resource/download/asset/a45a376a-39bb-4ee5-8935-8ce1ee865709.png
aa97cafe-78d6-4e81-88ce-bdcaf23cabbd.pdf	Tài liệu này tập trung vào các vấn đề liên quan đến kỹ thuật bảo mật, bao gồm các loại tấn công phổ biến và cách phòng chống. Nó đề cập đến các khía cạnh như bảo mật web, bảo mật mạng, và an toàn hệ thống, cùng với đó là việc đánh giá lỗ hổng và kiểm thử xâm nhập (pentest). Tài liệu còn trình bày về các phương pháp và công cụ để phát hiện, phân tích, và khắc phục các điểm yếu bảo mật trong phần mềm.	application/pdf	0	t	The document discusses various aspects of software and web security, including common vulnerabilities like SQL injection, XSS attacks, and insecure direct object references. It covers OWASP's top ten list, penetration testing, vulnerability assessment, and methods for identifying and mitigating security risks. It also provides examples of different types of attacks and methods used by attackers.	6ddd3712-f3c1-4c03-b46e-a4d18561ed90		Bach khoa University	[0.031152898,0.04791649,-0.017312646,0.0016044946,0.04377816,-0.04651413,-0.02507726,-0.011364804,0.06123766,0.04051662,-0.029424846,0.034854386,0.089929,0.034660168,-0.021687927,-0.043259136,0.0015823138,-0.049342517,0.008940057,-0.012359326,0.039460085,-0.007842859,0.016898422,-0.025667008,-0.0118747335,-0.012669325,0.035915643,0.03278403,-0.066551164,0.043963715,0.037522566,-0.011628177,0.016809653,0.036789324,0.044830076,0.007438842,-0.038413815,-0.0031584285,-0.008930438,0.02753662,0.048463054,0.043864597,0.015274387,-0.042886805,0.017745301,-0.04635739,0.04183913,0.019958893,-0.0045010056,-0.019191317,0.04599174,-0.014851522,0.065513335,0.031138139,-0.044801172,-0.020752834,0.02614025,0.012153096,-0.003707149,0.054524593,-0.018148815,0.015341003,-0.02831613,0.0639272,0.047572576,-0.01675371,0.011127875,-0.012877314,-0.05389954,-0.00528935,0.0132150045,-0.03277597,0.07233709,-0.026968965,-0.002567302,-0.013442177,-0.022747248,0.016386192,-0.02282444,-0.021795107,0.046484064,0.036300804,0.033730906,0.043623313,0.004162449,0.04033509,-0.010155238,0.027698591,