7915,-0.015732434,-0.04295965,0.027344542,0.02821258,0.004455121,-0.0050465222,0.026848262,-0.034310948,-0.043801203,0.023031982,0.032854814,-0.019153267,0.03064266,-0.033344004,-0.019206872,-0.009444822,0.016096318,-0.051816802,0.055621184,0.008163807,0.010462116,0.03442915,-0.02029238,0.060074072,-0.06338858,-0.04293225,-0.0047012055]	Machine Learning\nGenetic Algorithm\nFaculty of Computer Science and Engineering Hochiminh city University of Technology\nContents\n1. Introduction\n2. Genetic Algorithm\nIntroduction\nMotivation\n• Evolution is known to be a successful, robust method of nature\n• How do we search the space of hypotheses containing complex interacting parts, where the impact of each part on overall hypothesis is hard to model.\n• Computer programs are evolved to certain ﬁtness criteria.\n• Evolutionary computation = Genetic algorithms + genetic programming\nGenetic Algorithm\n• Learning as searching\n• Analogy to biological evolution\n• The best hypothesis is searched through several generations of hypotheses.\n• Next generation hypotheses are produced by mutating and recombining parts of the best current generation hypotheses\n• It is not recommended to search from general-to-speciﬁc or from simple-to-complex hypotheses.\nGenetic Algorithm\nGenetic Algorithms\nA Prototypical GA\n• Initialize population: P = randomly generated p hypotheses.\n• Evaluate ﬁtness: compute Fitness(h), for each h ∈ P\n• While maxh∈PFitness(h) < Fitnessthreshold do:\n• Create new generation\n• Evaluate ﬁtness\nNew Generation Creation\n• Selection\n• Crossover\n• Mutation\nNew Generation Creation\n• Selection:\nProbabilistically select (1 − r)p hypotheses of P to add to the new generation.\nThe selection probability of a hypothesis\nNew Generation Creation\n• Crossover:\n• Probabilistically select (r/2)p pairs of hypotheses from P according to Pr(h)\n• For each pair (h1,h2), produce two oﬀsprings by applying a Crossover operator.\n• Add all oﬀspring to the new generation.\nNew Generation Creation\n• Mutation:\n• Choose m percent of the added hypotheses with uniform distribution.\n• For each, invert one randomly selected bit in its representation.\nHypotheses Representation\n• A classiﬁcation rule as a bit string:\nIf expr(A1) ∧ ... ∧ expr(Ai ∧ ... ∧ expr(An) Then C = c\nHypothesis Representation\n• Example:\nIf Wind = Strong Then PlayTennis = Y es\nHypothesis Representation\n• A set of rules as concatenated bit strings:\n11]1 1]o1]of1]oofof1]0]1\nCrossover Operator\n• Single-point\n• Two-point\n• Uniform\nCrossover Operator\n• Single-point\nCrossover Operator\n• Two-point\nCrossover Operator\n• Uniform\nCrossover Operator\n• Variable-length bit strings\nFitness Function\n• Example:\ncorrect(h) = percent of all training examples correctly classiﬁed by hypothesis h\nInductive bias?\n• What is inductive bias?\n• Where is inductive bias in GA?\n	machine-learning genetic-algorithm evolution crossover mutation	http://localhost:8888/api/v1/resource/download/asset/4d54ea03-1fcf-4897-9e12-7058336d7903.png
6384a023-6ba7-47e6-86c0-13a8d2bf7213.pdf	Tài liệu này trình bày về Cây Cú pháp Trừu tượng (AST), một cấu trúc dữ liệu quan trọng trong lĩnh vực trình biên dịch và phân tích cú pháp. Nó so sánh AST với cây phân tích cú pháp, trình bày các khái niệm và cấu trúc AST, và cách xây dựng AST bằng Scala và ANTLR. Tài liệu cung cấp ví dụ về cách tạo AST cho các biểu thức và giải thích các phương pháp sử dụng trong quá trình này.	application/pdf	0	t	This document discusses the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST), its definition as a tree representation of source code's abstract syntax structure, and its differences from a concrete syntax tree (parse tree). It explores AST generation using tools like ANTLR, providing examples of AST structures in Scala and Python. The document also explains the role of visitors in AST generation, including the use of methods like `getChild` and `getChildCount`.	c910e9e5-7cb0-41f1-ba0a-402436a5d145		Bach khoa University	[-0.0064528813,0.03492352,-0.010556847,0.047416557,0.008907841,-0.010234704,-0.006524395,-0.04680467,0.011868849,0.013779516,-0.0021426296,0.007877101,0.08269262,0.03123182,-0.02695192,-0.063383676,-0.008634701,-0.019103361,0.035979122,-0.017944233,0.03164369,-0.041238673,0.0006871908,-0.0066230893,0.027315402,-0.01928071,0.031016937,0.02081041,-0.024864124,0.07026868,0.03815851,-0.046382196,0.022494158,0.02958921,0.02863335,-0.0072744475,-0.016316593,-0.061994135,-0.008109792,0.006349293,0.033866964,0.033170775,-0.00051534263,-0.056652687,0.0047258534,-0.0072087953,0.034718707,-0.0033369716,0.0060389163,-0.05634837,0.0353579,0.021488423,0.036309276,0.00907691,-0.09839684,-0.029276077,0.028923854,0.028606819,-0.059125014,0.026082255,0.024013082,0.037014376,0.009383427,0.0386553,0.073170446,-0.018283738,0.01235068,-0.019689191,-0.041511185,-0.0066110673,0.005458881,-0.031301606,0.04940536,-0.01602609,-0.040184636,-0.010781177,-0.042096153,-0.010803948,0.016871208,-0.03807883,0.030265003,0.024846956,-0.002857797,0.061293013,0.022446224,-0.