1089,0.06381949,-0.0072237737,0.0040290104,0.009824777,0.008260613,0.06624234,-0.07787902,-0.018937426,0.051066067,-0.028054614,0.029152755,0.0009386567,-0.038948044,0.0013705182,0.015864916,-0.031769015,-0.06480144,-0.0020195912,-0.016493727,-0.036920436,0.05459525,0.042671323,0.007552586,-0.020106072,0.021022305,-0.04747923,-0.04165897,0.01392493,0.023126068,-0.011625206,0.0196081,0.015778698,-0.021653341,-0.0335933,-0.015024499,-0.03134775,0.040850587,0.057725966,0.026988277,0.02727114,-0.03673894,0.0525691,-0.039541755,-0.017292516,0.015610673]	Deep Learning\nConvolutional Networks\nFaculty of Computer Science and Engineering Hochiminh city University of Technology\nContents\n1. Convolutional Networks\n2. Pooling\n3. Variants of the Basic Convolution Function\n4. Problems of Convolutional Networks\n5. Random or Unsupervised Features\n6. Case Study\nConvolutional Networks\n-\nConvolutional Networks\n• Convolutional networks (Convolutional Neural Networks, or CNNs: a specialized kind of neural network for processing data that has a known, grid-like topology\n• Convolution: a specialized kind of linear operation\n• CNNs: are simply NNs that use convolution in place of general matrix multiplication in at least one of their layers\nThe Convolution Operation\n• Signal processing\n• The convolution operation is typically denoted with an asterisk:\nThe Convolution Operation\nThe Convolution Operation\n• The continuous signal is unrealistic\n• Discrete convolution\nThe Convolution Operation\ne\nIn ML applications\n• The input is usually a multidimensional array of data\n• The kernel is usually a multidimensional array of parameters: are adapted by the learning algorithm\nThe Convolution Operation\nThe Convolution Operation\n• We often use convolutions over more than one axis at a time\n• If we have a 2D image I, we also need a 2D kernel K\n• Convolution is commutative:\nThe Convolution Operation\nThe Convolution Operation\n• Many ML frameworks implemented convolution as cross-correlation\n• Misleading\n• ML algorithm: learn the appropriate values of the kernel in the appropriate place\n• An algorithm based on convolution will learn a kernel that is ﬂipped relative to the kernel learned by an algorithm without ﬂipping.\nThe Motivation\n• Spare interactions\n• Parameter sharing\n• Equivariant representations\nThe Motivation\nSpare interactions\n• Traditional networks: every output unit interacts with every input unit\n• Large kernels → Small kernels\n• Memory eﬃciency\n• Computation requires fewer operations\n• Units in deeper layers may indirectly interact with larger portion of the input\nSparse connections due to small convolution kernel\nDense connections\nDeep Learning\nSparse connections due to small convolution kernel\nDense connections\nThe Motivation\nThe Motivation\nParameter Sharing\n• Using the same parameter for more than one function\n• Learn a set of parameters for every location\n• Does not reduce the runtime of forward propagation but the storage requirement\ne Motivation\nThe Motivation\nEquivariance\n• A function is equivariant means that if the input change, the output changes in the same way\n• Function f is equivariant to function g if f(g(x)) = g(f(x))\n• Convolution is not naturally equivariant to transformations (except translation)\nThe Motivation\nThe Motivation\nDeep Learning\nThe Motivation\nExamples\n• Input volume: 32x32x3\n• 10 5x5 ﬁlters with stride 1, pad 2\n• Output volume size: ?\nExamples\n• Input volume: 32x32x3\n• 10 5x5 ﬁlters with stride 1, pad 2\n• Number of parameters in this layer?\nConvolution Layer\n• Accepts a volume of size W1 × H1 × D1\n• Requires four hyperparameters:\n• Number of ﬁlters K\n• Their spatial extent F\n• The stride S\n• The amount of zero padding P\n• Produces a volume of size W2 × H2 × D2\n• W2 = (W1 − F + 2P)/S + 1\n• H2 = (H1 − F + 2P)/S + 1(i.e. width and height are computed equally by symmetry)\n• D2 = K\n• With parameter sharing, it introduces F · F · D1 weights per ﬁlter, for a total of (F · F · D1) · K weights and K biases.\n• In the output volume, the d-th depth slice (of size W2 × H2) is the result of performing a valid convolution of the d-th ﬁlter over the input volume with a stride of S, and then oﬀset by d-th bias.\nExamples\nPooling\nPooling\n• Pooling function: replaces the output of the net at a certain location with a summary statistic of the nearby outputs\n• The representation becomes approximately invariant to small translations of the input\n• We care whether some feature is present rather than where it is\nPooling\n• Adding an inﬁnitely strong prior: the function the layer learns must be invariant to small translations\n• What if we pool over the outputs of separately parameterized convolutions?\nPooling\nPooling\n• Pooling is essential for handling inputs of varying size\n• Complicate some kinds of neural network architectures that use top-down information (Boltzmann machines and autoencoders)\nPooling\nVariants of the Basic Convolution Function\nVariants of the Basic Convolution Function\n• The functions used in practice diﬀer slightly\n• An operation that consists of many applications of convolution in parallel\n• At each layer: extract many kinds of features, at many locations\n• The input is usually not just a grid of real values\nVariants of the Basic Convolution Function\n• Stride\n• Padding or no padding?\n• Valid convolution\n• Same convolution\n• Full convolution\n• Unshared convolutions\nVariants of the Basic Convolution Function\nVariants of the Basic Convolution Function\nnts of the Basic Convolution Func\nVariants of the Basic Convolution Function\n• Multiplication matrix is spare\n• Duplication in the matrix\nTypes of convolutions\n• Kernel size: deﬁnes the ﬁeld of view of the convolution.\n• Stride: deﬁnes the step size of the kernel when traversing the image.\n• Padding: deﬁnes how the border of a sample is handled.\nProblems of Convolutional Networks\nStructured Outputs\n• CNN can be used to output high-dimentional structured object\nData Types\n• 1D: audio waveform, skeleton animation data\n• 2D: audio data (FFT), color image data\n• 3D: Medical imaging data (CT scans), color video data\nRandom or Unsupervised Features\nRandom or Unsupervised Features\nTraining CNN\n• Learning the features\n• Unsupervised training\n• Used features that are not trained in a supervised fashion\nRandom or Unsupervised Features\nBasic strategies for obtaining convolution kernels\n• Initialize them randomly\n• Design by hand\n• Learn the kernels with an unsupervised criterion\nCase Study\nFull (simplified) AlexNet architecture\nFull (simpliﬁed) AlexNet architecture\nFull (simpliﬁed) AlexNet architecture\n• First use of ReLU\n• Used Norm layers (not common anymore) - heavy data augmentation\n• Dropout 0.5\n• Batch size 128\n• SGD Momentum 0.9\n• Learning rate 1e-2, reduced by 10 manually when val accuracy plateaus\n• L2 weight decay 5e-4\n• 7 CNN ensemble: 18.2% -¿ 15.4%\nModiﬁed version of AlexNet\nVGGNet\n[Simonyan and Zisserman, 2014]\n• Only 3x3 CONV stride 1, pad 1 and 2x2 MAX POOL stride 2\n• 11.2% top 5 error in ILSVRC 2013 → 7.3% top 5 error\n• Number of parameters?\nVGGNet\n• INPUT: [224x224x3] memory: 224*224*3=150K params: 0\n• CONV3-64: [224x224x64] memory: 224*224*64=3.2M params: (3*3*3)*64 = 1,728\n• CONV3-64: [224x224x64] memory: 224*224*64=3.2M params: (3*3*64)*64 = 36,864\n• POOL2: [112x112x64] memory: 112*112*64=800K params: 0\n• CONV3-128: [112x112x128] memory: 112*112*128=1.6M params: (3*3*64)*128 = 73,728\n• CONV3-128: [112x112x128] memory: 112*112*128=1.6M params: (3*3*128)*128 = 147,456\n• POOL2: [56x56x128] memory: 56*56*128=400K params: 0\n• CONV3-256: [56x56x256] memory: 56*56*256=800K params: (3*3*128)*256 = 294,912\n• CONV3-256: [56x56x256] memory: 56*56*256=800K params: (3*3*256)*256 = 589,824\n• CONV3-256: [56x56x256] memory: 56*56*256=800K params: (3*3*256)*256 = 589,824\n• POOL2: [28x28x256] memory: 28*28*256=200K params: 0\n• CONV3-512: [28x28x512] memory: 28*28*512=400K params: (3*3*256)*512 = 1,179,648\n• CONV3-512: [28x28x512] memory: 28*28*512=400K params: (3*3*512)*512 = 2,359,296\n• CONV3-512: [28x28x512] memory: 28*28*512=400K params: (3*3*512)*512 = 2,359,296\n• POOL2: [14x14x512] memory: 14*14*512=100K params: 0 (not counting biases)\n• CONV3-512: [14x14x512] memory: 14*14*512=100K\n• CONV3-512: [14x14x512] memory: 14*14*512=100K\n• CONV3-512: [14x14x512] memory: 14*14*512=100K\n• POOL2: [7x7x512] memory: 7*7*512=25K params: 0\n• FC: [1x1x4096] memory: 4096 params: 7*7*512*4096 = 102,760,448\n• FC: [1x1x4096] memory: 4096 params: 4096*4096 = 16,777,216\n• FC: [1x1x1000] memory: 1000 params: 4096*1000 = 4,096,000\nVGGNet\n• TOTAL memory: 24M * 4 bytes = 93MB / image (only forward! *2 for bwd)\n• TOTAL params: 138M parameters\nGooglLeNet\n(a) Inception module. naive version\n(b) Inception module with dimension reductions\nILSVRC 2014 winner (6.7% top 5 error)\nGoogLeNet\n• Fun features\n• Only 5 million params! (Removes FC layers completely)\n• Compared to AlexNet\n• 12X less params\n• 2x more compute - 6.67% (vs. 16.4%)\n52 / 52\n	deep-learning convolutional-networks cnns convolution pooling	http://localhost:8888/api/v1/resource/download/asset/51751510-010d-4315-ab34-0a22dd3bbaa8.png
962e437b-20b5-4218-add7-83521074215d.pdf	Tài liệu trình bày về các khái niệm cơ bản trong lập trình như: Name (Tên), Binding (Liên kết) và Scope (Phạm vi). Nó định nghĩa tên, các loại liên kết như Early Binding, Late Binding, và Static/Dynamic Binding, cũng như sự khác biệt giữa các kiểu Scope. Tài liệu cũng đề cập đến môi trường tham chiếu và các ví dụ về phạm vi trong ngôn ngữ lập trình.	application/pdf	0	t	The document discusses fundamental concepts in programming languages, including names, binding, and scope. It explains different binding times (early vs. late, static vs. dynamic), object lifetimes, and memory management aspects like dangling references and memory leaks.  Scope, both static and dynamic, is elaborated, along with the concept of referencing environments. The text also covers block structures, nested functions, parameter passing, default values, arbitrary arguments, and return statements. The content provides examples of static and dynamic scope, as well as the use of 'global' and 'nonlocal' keywords.	afad7410-3f1b-40cd-a13a-24e0a8501872		Bach khoa University	[0.020999258,0.049343012,-0.012843698,0.031860314,0.032264046,-0.058135673,-0.010334175,-0.025551833,0.029829983,0.0047230152,-0.017046351,0.012192353,0.12789068,0.045798246,-0.023358941,-0.057205472,0.008166675,-0.0064728456,0.034602422,-0.025289144,0.03402744,-0.022222806,0.029894324,-0.047068637,0.0059150085,-0.03151326,0.029444983,0.024019293,-0.01912906,0.074430384,0.026013957,-0.028311396,0.008197635,0.037961923,0.034378037,-0.0039525414,0.0066232407,-0.03938245,0.014810412,0.0033911162,0.015889803,0.011208017,0.03286032,-0.053737104,0.026655871,-0.020150404,0.036596566,0.010357341,0.0064057275,-0.057175443,0.03680544,0.02187955,0.04254287,0.017056286,-0.07936986,-0.047981154,0.016263038,0.0018672883,-0.019994076,0.021903748,-0.024193523,0.04282111,-0.008896696,0.049734537,0.052192777,-0.03820657,0.0003246541,-0.032534618,-0.012716557,0.0052368287,-0.0021884604,0.0012228944,0.07432619,-