47691427,-0.037740737,0.05747823,-0.0051757554,-0.049107943,0.06025422,0.00080695044,-0.011390156,0.01861452,0.019207386,0.04304577,-0.062888294,-0.015265104,0.04503711,-0.029434443,0.021209188,0.00023078805,-0.047105692,0.010725799,0.015751634,-0.016547214,-0.07834368,0.00681001,-0.018643001,-0.025359334,0.05483927,0.03337218,0.018302187,-0.013625902,0.020692319,-0.035778835,-0.05942582,0.018411217,0.025329363,-0.000601821,0.008376038,0.0036750375,-0.0353753,0.005564635,0.013348583,-0.05850742,0.04404058,0.02220636,0.041802272,0.021558603,-0.010429418,0.047559656,-0.053593297,-0.023519143,0.03882789]	Deep Learning\nOptimization for Training Deep Models\nFaculty of Computer Science and Engineering Hochiminh city University of Technology\nContents\n1. Learning vs. Pure Optimization\n2. Challenges in Neural Network Optimization\n3. Basic Algorithms\n4. Parameter Initialization Strategies\n5. Algorithms with Adaptive Learning Rates\n6. Approximate Second-Order Methods\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\n—\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\n• Machine learning usually acts indirectly\n• Typically, the cost function can be written as an average over the training set, such as\nwhere L is the per-example loss function, f(x;θ) is the predicted output when the input is x, and ˆpdata is the empirical distribution. In the supervised learning case, y is the target output.\n• → Objective function with respect to the training set\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\n1. Empirical Risk Minimization\n• The goal of a ML algorithm is to reduce the expected generalization error → risk.\n• If we knew the true distribution pdata(x,y), risk minimization would be an optimization task solvable by an optimization algorithm\n• We only have a training set of samples → we have a machine learning problem\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\n• Minimize the expected loss on the training set\n• Minimize the empirical risk\nwhere m is the number of training examples.\n• DL: rarely use empirical risk minimization!\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\n2. Surrogate Loss Functions and Early Stopping\n• Sometimes, the loss function is not one that can be optimized eﬃciently\n• Optimizes a surrogate loss function: acts as a proxy but has advantages\n• Minimizes a surrogate loss function but halts when a convergence criterion based on early stopping is satisﬁed\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\n3. Batch and Minibatch Algorithms\n• Objective function usually decomposes as a sum over the training examples\n• Optimization: update the parameters based on an expected value of the cost function estimated using only a subset of the full cost function\n• Batch or deterministic gradient methods: process all of the training examples simultaneously in a large batch\n• Stochastic (or online methods): use only a single example at a time\n• Most algorithms used for DL fall somewhere in between, using more than one but less than all of the training examples\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\nMinibatch sizes:\n• Larger batches: provide a more accurate estimate of the gradient, but with less than linear returns.\n• Multicore architectures are usually underutilized by extremely small batches\n• Using some absolute minimum batch size: no reduction in the time to process a minibatch\nLearning vs. Pure Optimization\n• Batch size deﬁnes performance\n• Small batch size vs. regularizing → tradeoﬀ?\n• Mini bacthes should be selected randomly!\nChallenges in Neural Network Optimization\n&\nIll-Conditioning\n• Causing SGD to get “stuck”: even very small steps increase the cost function\n• Given the Taylor expansion of cost function:\na gradient descent step —eg will add to the cost:\n• Convex optimization: local minima = global minima\n• What about ﬂat area?\n• We have reached a good solution if we ﬁnd a critical point of any kind\n• Non-convex functions (NN): many local minima\nLocal Minima\n• Model identiﬁability problem: a suﬃciently large training set can rule out all but one setting of the model’s parameters\n• Models with latent variables are often not identiﬁable (e.g. weight space symmetry)\nLocal minima\n• Local minima can be problematic if they have high cost in comparison to the global minimum\n• Serious problem for gradient-based optimization algorithms\nPlateaus, Saddle Points and Other Flat Regions\n• Saddle point: point with zero gradient\n• A local minimum along one cross-section of the cost function and a local maximum along another cross-section\n• In low-dimensional spaces: local minima are common\n• In higher dimensional spaces: local minima are rare and saddle points are more common\nPlateaus, Saddle Points and Other Flat Regions\nZero gradient, and Hessian with...\nPlateaus, Saddle Points and Other Flat Regions\n• Important properties of many random functions: eigenvalues of the Hessian become more likely to be positive as we reach regions of lower cost\n• Critical points with high cost are far more likely to be saddle points\n• Critical points with extremely high cost are more likely to be local maxima\n• Gradient descent empirically seems to be able to escape saddle points in many cases\nCliﬀs and Exploding Gradients\n• NN with many layers: often have extremely steep regions resembling cliﬀs\n• Reason: multiplication of several large weights together\n• The gradient update step can move the parameters extremely far, usually jumping oﬀ of the cliﬀ structure altogether\nCliﬀs and Exploding Gradients\nCliﬀs and Exploding Gradients\n• Cliﬀ structures are most common in the cost functions for RNN\n• Involve a multiplication of many factors, with one factor for each time step\n• Long temporal sequences → extreme amount of multiplication\nBasic Algorithms\nStochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)\nStochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)\n• SGD gradient estimator introduces a source of noise: the random sampling of m training examples\n• Does not vanish!\nCrucial parameter: learning rate ¢\n• → Should be adapted over time\n• A suﬃcient condition to guarantee convergence\nStochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)\n• Common practice: decay the learning rate linearly until iteration τ\nwith a =\nk k\ne After iteration T, leave € constant\n• Computation time per update does not grow with the number of training examples\nMomentum\n• Accelerate learning: high curvature, small but consistent gradients, or noisy gradients\n• Accumulate an exponentially decaying moving average of past gradients and continues to move in their direction\nMomentum\nDeep Learning\nParameter Initialization Strategies\nParameter Initialization Strategies\n• Modern initialization strategies: simple and heuristic\n• The initial parameters need to “break symmetry” between diﬀerent units\n• Set the biases for each unit to heuristically chosen constants\n• Extra parameters (e.g. parameters encoding the conditional variance of a prediction) are usually set to heuristically chosen constants\nParameter Initialization Strategies\n• Initialize all the weights in the model to values drawn randomly from a Gaussian or uniform distribution\n• The scale of the initial distribution has a large eﬀect\n• The outcome of the optimization procedure\n• The ability of the network to generalize\nParameter Initialization Strategies\n• Larger initial weights → stronger symmetry breaking eﬀect, helping to avoid redundant units\n• Too large initial weights → exploding values during forward propagation or back-propagation\n• How to choose?\n• Optimization: the weights should be large enough to propagate information successfully\n• Regularization: encourage making the weights smaller\nParameter Initialization Strategies\nChoosing bias\n• If a bias is for an output unit\n• Initialize the bias to obtain the right marginal statistics of the output\n• Assume that the initial weights are small enough that the output of the unit is determined only by the bias\n• Set the bias to the inverse of the activation function applied to the marginal statistics of the output in the training set\n• Sometimes we may want to choose the bias to avoid causing too much saturation at initialization\n• When a unit controls whether other units are able to participate in a function\n30/\nAlgorithms with Adaptive Learning Rates\nAdaGrad\n• Individually adapts the learning rates of all model parameters: scaling them inversely proportional to the square root of the sum of all of their historical squared values\n• Decreasing rate depends on partial derivative of parameter\n• The net eﬀect is greater progress in the more gently sloped directions of parameter space\nRMSProp\n• Modify AdaGrad to perform better in the non-convex setting\n• Change the gradient accumulation into an exponentially weighted moving average\n• Compared to AdaGrad: introduces a new hyperparameter, ρ, that controls the length scale of the moving average\n• “Adam” derives from the phrase “adaptive moments”\n• A variant on the combination of RMSProp and momentum with a few important distinctions\nRequire:\nStep s € (Suggested default: 0.001)\nRequire: Exponential decay rates for moment estimates, 1 and p in [0,1). (Suggested defaults: 0.9 and 0.999 respectively)\nRequire: Small constant § used for numerical stabilization (Suggested default: 10-5)\nRequire: Initial parameters 6\nInitialize 1st and 2nd moment variables s = 0, 7 = 0\nInitialize time step ¢ = 0\nwhile stopping criterion not met do\nSample a minibatch of m examples from the training set {V, ..., @™} with corresponding targets y().\nCompute gradient: g « } Vo ¥ L(f(a;),y) tet+1\nUpdate biased first moment estimate: s « p1s+ (1 — py),\nUpdate biased second moment estimate: ¢ por+ (1 — p2)g @ g\nCorrect bias in first moment:\nCorrect bias in second moment:\n§ « 1\n7 " i\nCompute update:\napplied element-wis A0 = —c ,\nApply update:\n6 « 6 + A8\nend while\n• RMSProp: lacks the correction factor → second-order moment estimate may have high bias early in training\n• Adam: fairly robust to the choice of hyperparameters\nChoosing the Right Optimization Algorithm\n• Which algorithm should one choose?\n• SGD, SGD with momentum, RMSProp, RMSProp with momentum, AdaDelta, Adam\n• Your choice is depend largely on the familiarity with the algorithm (for ease of hyperparameter tuning)\n38\nApproximate Second-Order Methods\nNewton’s Method\n• An optimization scheme based on using a second-order Taylor series expansion to approximate J(θ) near some point θ0\nwhere H is the Hessian of J with respect to θ evaluated at θ0\n• Newton parameter update rule:\n40/\nConjugate Gradients\n• Eﬃciently avoid the calculation of the inverse Hessian by iteratively descending conjugate directions\n• Seek to ﬁnd a search direction that is conjugate to the previous line search direction, i.e. it will not undo progress made in that direction\n• At training iteration t, the next search direction dt takes the form:\n• βt is a coeﬃcient whose magnitude controls how much of the direction, dt−1, we should add back to the current search direction\nConjugate Gradients\no d; and d;_1, are defined as conjugate if d H(J)d;—1 = 0\n• Can we calculate the conjugate directions without resorting to these calculations?\nTwo directions, d; and d;_1, are defined as conjugate if H =0\nConjugate Gradients\nTwo popular methods for computing the βt:\n• Fletcher-Reeves:\n• Polak-Ribi`ere:\n• The Broyden–Fletcher–Goldfarb–Shanno (BFGS): bring some of the advantages of Newton’s method without the computational burden\n• BFGS is similar to CG\n• Newton’s update is given by\nwhere H is the Hessian of J with respect to θ evaluated at θ0\n→ approximate the inverse with a matrix Mt that is iteratively reﬁned by low rank updates to become a better approximation of H−1\n• Unlike CG, the success of BFGS is not heavily dependent on the line search ﬁnding a point very close to the true minimum along the line\n	deep-learning optimization training algorithms model	http://localhost:8888/api/v1/resource/download/asset/ff303ca1-d9b4-4984-bce9-f9b7c38f02f9.png
64b26db8-9971-48fc-ad0c-3977d89a4817.pdf	Tài liệu này giới thiệu về Machine Learning, tập trung vào các mô hình đồ thị. Nội dung bao gồm các khái niệm về Bayesian Networks, Naive Bayes Classifier và Hidden Markov Models (HMM). Phần tóm tắt nhấn mạnh các ứng dụng và đặc điểm của từng mô hình, bao gồm cả việc áp dụng HMM vào bài toán nhận dạng thực thể.	application/pdf	0	t	The document discusses Machine Learning, focusing on Graphical Models, specifically Bayesian Networks, Naive Bayes Classifier, and Hidden Markov Models (HMMs). It covers the concepts of conditional independence, factorization, generative vs. discriminative models, and algorithms like Forward and Viterbi.  The document also explores HMM training (supervised and unsupervised), its application in Named Entity Recognition (NER) using BIO notation, and includes practical exercises.	ad307158-a133-4b6d-8a0a-83bf1242855b		Bach khoa University	[0.028380891,0.05607602,-0.0067735733,0.0150151085,0.038344193,-0.06057369,-0.028658453,-0.019007359,0.019681232,0.02424686,-0.035687827,-0.0033507645,0.11496522,0.04970494,-0.025142623,-0.042505924,0.03161825,-0.013669606,0.02288444,-0.022703018,0.03554566,-0.025079185,0.072087035,-0.009626309,-0.0032473817,-0.021458687,0.050537728,0.02615098,-0.021194229,0.055072363,0.024253711,-0.03717419,0.021727806,0.0302713,0.047827452,0.024966814,-0.014820254,-0.045415167,0.011333116,0.049479675,0.02585273,0.035420667,0.025834681,-0.05691857,0.022304617,-0.038193613,0.029159801,0.024496634,0.00015220803,-0.040839117,0.03290796,0.02651825,0.06081281,0.03045094,-0.06805998,-0.011437516,0.028409785,0.040327407,-0.025680067,0.041688558,-0.0135951,0.032089066,-0.010546288,0.0255214,0.03599854,-0.028317254,0.017235555,-0.06525104,0.0004992299,-0.029364482,0.019653728,-0.020583952,0.085522294,-0.018410072,-0.022157624,0.013780378,-0.014961913,0.025659444,0.013165086,-0.019609906,0.058265977,0.012694843,0.012039962,0.024626601,0.035333753,-0.02696915,-0.022096679,0.028889142,0.014631138