 p(C,A)\n• It can generate any distribution on C and A.\nIn contrast to a discriminative model (e.g., CRF)\n• Conditional distribution: P(C|A)\n• It discriminates C given A\nHidden Markov Models\nHidden Markov Models\n• Introduction\n• Example\n• Independence assumptions\n• Forward algorithm\n• Viterbi algorithm\n• Training\n• Application to NER\nHidden Markov Models\n• One of the most popular graphical models.\n• Dynamic extension of Bayesian networks.\n• Sequential extension of Naive Bayes classiﬁer.\nHidden Markov Models\nExample:\n• Your possible looking prior to the exam: (tired,hungover,scared,fine)\nHidden Markov Models\nExample:\n• Your possible looking prior to the exam: (tired,hungover,scared,fine)\n• Your possible activity last night: (TV,pub,party,study)\nHidden Markov Models\nExample:\n• Your possible looking prior to the exam: (tired,hungover,scared,fine)\n• Your possible activity last night: (TV,pub,party,study)\n• Given the sequence of observations of your looking, guess what you did in previous nights.\nHidden Markov Models\nExample:\n• Your possible looking prior to the exam: (tired,hungover,scared,fine)\n• Your possible activity last night: (TV,pub,party,study)\n• Given the sequence of observations of your looking, guess what you did in previous nights.\nA model:\nHidden Markov Models\nExample:\n• Your possible looking prior to the exam: (tired,hungover,scared,fine)\n• Your possible activity last night: (TV,pub,party,study)\n• Given the sequence of observations of your looking, guess what you did in previous nights.\nA model:\n• Your looking depends on what you did in the night before.\nHidden Markov Models\nExample:\n• Your possible looking prior to the exam: (tired,hungover,scared,fine)\n• Your possible activity last night: (TV,pub,party,study)\n• Given the sequence of observations of your looking, guess what you did in previous nights.\nA model:\n• Your looking depends on what you did in the night before.\n• Your activity in a night depends on what you did in some previous nights.\nHidden Markov Models\n• A ﬁnite set of possible observations.\n• A ﬁnite set of possible hidden states.\n• To predict the most probable sequence of underlying stats {y1,y2,...,yT} for a given sequence of observations {x1,x2,...,xT}\nHidden Markov Models\nHidden Markov Models\nLecturer: Duc Dung Nguyen,\nMarsland, S. (2009) Machine Learning: An Algorithmic Perspective.\n38\nHMM conditional independence assumptions:\n• State at time t depends only on state at time t − 1.\n• Observation at time t depends only on state at time t.\nHidden Markov Models\nHMM is a generative model:\n• Joint distributions:\nHMM is a generative model:\n• Joint distributions:\n• It can generate any distribution on Y and X\nHidden Markov Models\nHMM is a generative model:\n• Joint distributions:\n• It can generate any distribution on Y and X\nIn contrast to a discriminative model (e.g., CRF):\nForward algorithm:\n• To compute the joint probability of the state at the time t being yt and the sequence of observations in the ﬁrst t steps being {x1,x2,...,xt}:\nForward algorithm:\n• To compute the joint probability of the state at the time t being yt and the sequence of observations in the ﬁrst t steps being {x1,x2,...,xt}:\n• Bayes’ theorem gives:\nForward algorithm:\n• To compute the joint probability of the state at the time t being yt and the sequence of observations in the ﬁrst t steps being {x1,x2,...,xt}:\n• Bayes’ theorem gives:\n• The highest αt(yt) is the most likely yt would be given the same {x1,x2,...,xt}.\nHidden Markov Models\nForward algorithm\nHidden Markov Models\nForward algorithm\nForward algorithm:\nMachine Learning\nHidden Markov Models\nViterbi algorithm:\n• To compute the most probable sequence of states {y1,y2,...,yT} given a sequence of observations {x1,x2,...,xT}:\nViterbi algorithm:\n• To compute the most probable sequence of states {y1,y2,...,yT} given a sequence of observations {x1,x2,...,xT}:\n• Viterbi algorithm:\n• Viterbi algorithm:\ne Dynamic programming:\n• Compute\n• For each t from 2 to T, and for each state yt, compute:\n• Select\ne Dynamic programming:\n• Compute\ne Dynamic programming:\n• Compute\n• For each t from 2 to T, and for each state yt, compute:\ne Dynamic programming:\n• Compute\n• For each t from 2 to T, and for each state yt, compute:\n• Select\ne Dynamic programming:\n• Compute\n• For each t from 2 to T, and for each state yt, compute:\n• Select\n• Could the results from the forward algorithm be used for Viterbi algorithm?\nMachine Learning\nHidden Markov Models\nMachine Learning\nCould the results from the forward algorithm be used for Viterbi algorithm?\nHidden Markov Models\nTraining HMMs:\n• Topology is designed beforehand.\n• Parameters to be learned: emission and transition probabilities.\n• Supervised or unsupervised training.\nHidden Markov Models\nSupervised learning:\n• Training data: paired sequences of states and observations (y1,y2,...,yT,x1,x2,...,xt)\n• p(yi) = number of sequences starting with yi/number of all sequences.\n• p(yj|yi) = number of (yi,yj)’s / number of all (yi,y)’s\n• p(xj|yi) = number of (yi,xj)’s / number of all (yi,x)’s\nSupervised learning example:\n\n\n=== [BẢNG SỐ LIỆU] ===\nFFFBFF BFFBFF FFBFFF FFFFBF HHTHTH THTHTH THHTTH THTTTH BFFFBF FFFBBF BFFFFF BFBFFF THHTHT HHTHHT HHTTHT HTTTHH\n======================\nUnsupervised learning:\n• Only observation sequences are available\n\n\n=== [BẢNG SỐ LIỆU] ===\nFFFRFF —BEFREFF —FE —FEFRE HHTHTH THTHTH THHTTH THTTTH BFFFBF—FFFBBF—BFFFFF—BFBFFF THHTHT HHTHHT HHTTHT HTTTHH\n======================\nUnsupervised learning:\n• Only observation sequences are available\n\n\n=== [BẢNG SỐ LIỆU] ===\nFFFRFF —BEFREFF —FE —FEFRE HHTHTH THTHTH THHTTH THTTTH BFFFBF—FFFBBF—BFFFFF—BFBFFF THHTHT HHTHHT HHTTHT HTTTHH\n======================\n• Iterative improvement of model parameters.\n• How?\nUnsupervised learning:\n• Initialize estimated parameters\nUnsupervised learning:\n• Initialize estimated parameters\n• For each observation sequence, compute the most probable state sequence, using Viterbi algorithm.\nUnsupervised learning:\n• Initialize estimated parameters\n• For each observation sequence, compute the most probable state sequence, using Viterbi algorithm.\n• Update the parameters using supervised learning on obtained paired state-observation sequences.\nUnsupervised learning:\n• Initialize estimated parameters\n• For each observation sequence, compute the most probable state sequence, using Viterbi algorithm.\n• Update the parameters using supervised learning on obtained paired state-observation sequences.\n• Repeat it until convergence.\nHidden Markov Models\nApplication to NER:\n• Example: "Facebook CEO Zuckerberg visited Vietnam".\nHidden Markov Models\nApplication to NER:\n• Example: "Facebook CEO Zuckerberg visited Vietnam".\n• States = Class labels\nHidden Markov Models\nApplication to NER:\n• Example: "Facebook CEO Zuckerberg visited Vietnam".\n• States = Class labels\n• Observations = Words + Features\nHidden Markov Models\nApplication to NER:\nMachine Learning\nHidden Markov Models\nApplication to NER:\n• What if a name is a multi-word phrase?\n• Example: "...John von Neumann is ..."\n• BIO notation: {B-PER, I-PER, B-ORG, I-ORG, B-LOC, I-LOC, B-MISC, I-MISC, O}\n• Readings\n• Marsland, S. (2009) Machine learning:An algorithmic perspective. Chapter 15 (graphical models).\n• Bikel, D. M. etal. (1997) Nymble: a high performance learning name-ﬁnder.\ne HW\n• Apply Viterbi algorithm to ﬁnd the most probable 3-state sequence in the looking-activity example in the lecture.\n• Write a program to carry out the unsupervised learning example for HMM in the lecture. Discuss on the result, in particular the convergence of the process.\n	machine-learning graphical-models bayesian-networks hmm naive-bayes	http://localhost:8888/api/v1/resource/download/asset/ebe4c06e-b8e0-4d92-9040-aa4e27ae907d.png
